     "question": "Explain the different types of soil found in India and their distribution.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "India has a variety of soil types due to its diverse climate, topography, and vegetation. The major soil types include alluvial soil, black soil, red soil, laterite soil, arid soil, and forest soil. Alluvial soil is found in the Indo-Gangetic plains and river valleys, formed by the deposition of sediments by rivers. It is fertile and ideal for agriculture. Black soil, also known as regur soil, is found in the Deccan Plateau, particularly in Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, and Gujarat. It is rich in calcium carbonate, magnesium, iron, and lime, making it suitable for cotton cultivation. Red soil is found in eastern and southern India, particularly in Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, and Odisha. It is rich in iron oxide but lacks nitrogen, phosphorus, and lime. Laterite soil is found in areas with high rainfall, such as the Western Ghats and parts of the Northeast. It is rich in iron and aluminum but lacks fertility. Arid soil is found in arid and semi-arid regions, such as Rajasthan and parts of Gujarat. It is sandy and saline, with low organic matter. Forest soil is found in forested areas, such as the Himalayas and the Northeast. It is rich in organic matter and supports dense vegetation."
