     "question": "Explain the different types of soils found in India and their distribution.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "India has diverse soil types due to varied climatic conditions and geological formations. The major soil types are: 1) Alluvial Soil: Found in the northern plains, deposited by rivers like Ganga, Brahmaputra, and Indus. It is fertile and ideal for agriculture. 2) Black Soil: Also known as regur soil, found in Deccan Plateau, rich in calcium, magnesium, iron, and aluminum. Suitable for cotton cultivation. 3) Red Soil: Predominant in eastern and southern India, rich in iron oxide, less fertile but can be improved with fertilizers. 4) Laterite Soil: Found in high rainfall areas like Western Ghats and Chotanagpur Plateau, rich in iron and aluminum but poor in nutrients. 5) Desert Soil: Found in arid regions of Rajasthan, sandy and saline, with low organic matter. 6) Mountain Soil: Found in hilly areas, thin and rocky, varies with altitude. These soils support different agricultural activities and vegetation based on their properties and distribution."
