     "question": "Explain the different types of soil found in India and their distribution.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "India has diverse soil types due to varied climatic conditions and geological formations. The major soil types include: Alluvial soils, found in the Northern Plains and river valleys, are fertile and support intensive agriculture. Black soils (regur) are rich in calcium, magnesium, potassium and lime, found in Deccan Plateau, ideal for cotton cultivation. Red soils, derived from crystalline and metamorphic rocks, cover parts of Eastern and Southern India, less fertile but used for growing millets, pulses, and oilseeds. Laterite soils, found in high rainfall areas, are rich in iron and aluminum but poor in nitrogen and phosphorus. Desert soils, found in arid regions, are sandy and saline. Mountain soils, found in hilly areas, are shallow and rich in organic matter. These soils support various agricultural activities and are crucial for India's economy."
