     "answer": "India has three main cropping seasons: Kharif, Rabi, and Zaid. Kharif season starts with the onset of monsoon (June-July) and crops are harvested by September-October. Major Kharif crops include rice, maize, cotton, jute, and sugarcane. Rabi season starts in winter (October-November) and crops are harvested by March-April. Major Rabi crops include wheat, barley, mustard, and gram. Zaid season is a short summer season between Rabi and Kharif (March to June). Major Zaid crops include watermelon, cucumber, and vegetables. These seasons are determined by monsoon patterns and temperature variations across the country."
