     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "India has diverse soil types due to its varied climatic conditions and geological formations. The major soil types include:\n\n1. Alluvial soil: Found in the northern plains, river valleys, and coastal regions. It is fertile, rich in minerals, and ideal for agriculture.\n\n2. Black soil: Also known as regur soil, found in Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, and Gujarat. It is rich in calcium, magnesium, and iron, suitable for cotton cultivation.\n\n3. Red soil: Found in eastern and southern India, particularly in the Deccan Plateau. It is rich in iron oxides but lacks nitrogen and phosphorus.\n\n4. Laterite soil: Found in tropical regions with heavy rainfall, such as the eastern and western coastal areas. It is rich in iron and aluminum but poor in fertility.\n\n5. Desert soil: Found in arid regions like Rajasthan and parts of Gujarat. It is sandy and saline, with low organic matter.\n\n6. Mountain soil: Found in the Himalayan region and other mountainous areas. It is rich in organic matter but varies in texture and composition."
