     "answer": "Rainwater harvesting is the collection and storage of rainwater for later use. It is an ancient practice that has gained renewed importance due to increasing water scarcity and environmental concerns.\n\nImportance:\n1. Recharges groundwater levels, reducing dependence on borewells.\n2. Provides water during dry periods, especially in water-scarce regions.\n3. Reduces flooding in urban areas by managing stormwater runoff.\n4. Improves water quality by reducing contamination from surface sources.\n\nMethods:\n1. Rooftop harvesting: Rainwater from rooftops is collected through pipes and stored in tanks or underground sumps.\n2. Surface runoff harvesting: Rainwater from open areas is collected in ponds or tanks.\n3. Check dams and percolation tanks: Structures built to capture and store rainwater, allowing it to percolate into the ground.\n4. Traditional methods like johads, kuls, and bawris in Rajasthan and other arid regions."
