     "question": "Explain the different types of soil found in India and their distribution.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "India has diverse types of soils due to variations in climate, vegetation, and parent material. The major soil types are: 1. Alluvial soils: Found in the Northern Plains, river valleys, and coastal plains. These are fertile soils deposited by rivers. 2. Black soils: Also known as regur soils, found in the Deccan Plateau. These soils are rich in calcium, magnesium, and iron but poor in nitrogen. 3. Red soils: Found in Eastern and Southern India, these soils are rich in iron but poor in nitrogen, phosphorus, and lime. 4. Laterite soils: Found in high rainfall areas of the Eastern and Western Ghats, these soils are rich in iron and aluminum but poor in nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium. 5. Desert soils: Found in arid and semi-arid regions of Rajasthan, these soils are sandy and saline. 6. Forest soils: Found in forested areas, these soils are rich in organic matter. 7. Mountain soils: Found in the Himalayan region, these soils are immature and vary in texture based on altitude."
