     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The Green Revolution was a period of agricultural transformation in India during the 1960s and 1970s, characterized by the introduction of high-yielding varieties of seeds, chemical fertilizers, pesticides, and modern agricultural techniques. Its significance includes: 1. Increased food production: The Green Revolution significantly increased the production of wheat and rice, making India self-sufficient in food grains. 2. Higher agricultural productivity: New farming techniques and inputs led to higher yields per hectare. 3. Economic growth: The increased agricultural production contributed to economic growth and reduced dependence on food imports. 4. Rural development: The Green Revolution created employment opportunities in rural areas and improved the standard of living for farmers. However, it also led to environmental issues such as soil degradation, water pollution, and loss of biodiversity. Despite these challenges, the Green Revolution played a crucial role in transforming India from a food-deficient country to a food-surplus one."
